          "session_id": "full-g-md-010",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Burning when I pass urine, plus a temperature and back pain since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what does guidance describe for treatment?",
          "answer": "**Seek medical assessment today.** Burning when you pass urine, plus fever and back pain, is not a routine symptom pattern. The reference material says fever and back or flank pain with urinary symptoms move it out of the routine category (*Urinary tract infection*, General clinical reference). Please contact a doctor, urgent care, or a same-day service today rather than waiting.\n\n**Go to emergency care now, or call your local emergency number, if you have any of these:**\n- Confusion, unusual drowsiness, or being hard to wake\n- Fainting, or feeling faint when you stand\n- Trouble breathing, or a racing heartbeat\n- Shaking chills, or a fever that is getting worse\n- Vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down\n- Very little urine passed\n\nThe sepsis guidance (red-flag-sepsis) warns against waiting to see whether a fever settles overnight, and says a normal or low temperature is not reassuring. It did not include the specific sepsis warning signs in the sections I could access, so this list is not complete.\n\nIf you might be pregnant, tell the clinician at once, because urinary infections in pregnancy need prompt review. I could not retrieve pregnancy-specific guidance for this pattern.\n\nThese symptoms together fit an infection that may have reached the kidney area. That is a reason for prompt assessment, not a diagnosis. Only a clinician can examine you and test a urine sample, and possibly blood, to find out what is happening. Ask whether your symptoms could mean a kidney infection and whether you need to be seen in person today.\n\n**Treatment:** I could not find treatment guidance for suspected kidney involvement. The one relevant document (guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment) covers only simple bladder infections in healthy, non-pregnant adults, and states that suspected kidney involvement is managed differently, with different antibiotic choices and durations. So I can't tell you the usual first-line options, how long treatment lasts, or where authorities disagree. Please ask the clinician who sees you, and do not take any medicine for this without medical advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 17916.90629699997,
